Halide perovskites are extraordinary solar absorbers, but the best-performing
compositions contain lead and degrade in air. We ask a more general question: *which* structural
and electronic features actually give these materials their defect tolerance, and can we
reproduce them in safer chemistries?

Our work spans bismuth and antimony chalcogenides and chalcohalides — NaBiS2, AgBiS2, BiSBr,
Sb2S3, CuSbSe2 — using cation disorder, ligand chemistry and dimensionality as design handles.
The aim is a set of rules for choosing new absorbers, rather than a single material.
